  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant precipitation persisting tonight,\nwith light wintry precipitation remaining possible over \nMoosehead and North Woods areas tonight. The wintry \nprecipitation is not expected to impact travel, and flooding is \nnot expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n1022 sfc high sitting over ern New Brunswick with ridge axis \nextending along the Downeast coast. Low pressure located over the \nGreat Lakes with warm front nosing into nrn New England has resulted \nin chilly air remaining locked in over CWA. 18z raob from KCAR has \nlowest 5kft still dry with bulk of the rainfall over southern areas. \nSnow is falling in the Millinocket area and up into the North Woods \nas of 17z. Expect that airmass will saturate down over the next\n1-2 hours with a cold rain likely for most of the area. The \nexception to this continues to be the North Woods and St. John \nValley where light snow or a rain/snow mix expected to continue \nlate this afternoon through the evening with around an inch over\nthe highest elevations but little or no accumulation elsewhere.\n\nOverall QPF amounts will average around 0.70-0.80 inches over \nthe far north and around 1 inch over central areas. The Downeast\ncoast forecast to see between 1.25-1.50 inches as triple point \ndevelops late tonight and moves along the Downeast coast. Cannot\nrule out nuisance and small ponding of water on area roadways \nwith drains being clogged with leaves. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread rain is likely again Friday/Friday\nnight. Chance of accumulating wintry precipitation for northern\nareas and strong winds Friday night into Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong low pressure system is expected to develop over the\nGreat Lakes, then move west towards the state this weekend. This\nsystem is expected to bring widespread precip to the region\nFriday. As of this update, the ensemble models continue to show\na split between a northern and southern track. Specifically, the\nGEFS seems to be the outlier with the northern track. If the\nsouthern track across the Downeast coast and Gulf of Maine\nhappens, then we can expect a more easterly wind will limit the\namount of warm air transport, allowing for more cold air\nadvection and snow development. Nevertheless, once the system\nexits on Saturday, the approaching high pressure will tighten\npressure gradients causing NW winds to increase. Expect gusty\nwinds possibly up to 35 mph.",
